<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catharism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Albigensians, Cathars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cathars were a Christian dualist movement of the 12th and 13th centuries, strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, who believed the material world was the creation of an evil principle. Led by ascetic 'perfects', they rejected the sacraments and hierarchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> against them in 1209, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> had largely eliminated them by the early 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catharism.jpg" alt="Catharism" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Berruguete · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berruguete_ordeal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Few Cathar texts survive, and much of what is known of Cathar thought comes from Catholic denunciations of the movement. Most historians of the 20th century agreed that its beliefs came from the East, from the weakening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, along the trade routes, and spread from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The movement appears to have been strongly influenced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a> of the First Bulgarian Empire, and may have begun among followers of the Paulician movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> who were resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. The name Cathar, from the Greek for the pure ones, was coined by Catholic theologians; in their own texts the believers called themselves Good Men, Good Women or Good Christians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>According to Catholic sources, Cathars held that there were two Gods, a good God of the spiritual realm and an evil god who made the physical world and whom they identified with Satan. Some communities held a mitigated dualism, in which Satan had once been the true God's servant before he rebelled; others, probably a majority over time, held an absolute dualism of two equal principles. Human souls were thought to be sexless spirits trapped in matter, reincarnated until they were freed through the consolamentum, a rite that removed sin and made the believer a Perfect. The Cathars honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> but denied his physical incarnation and resurrection.</p><p>The Perfecti lived in austerity, avoided animal food and anything produced by sexual reproduction, and travelled in pairs to minister to the people. Ordinary believers were called credentes. Women could be Perfect and administer the consolamentum. By about 1140 a liturgy and doctrine had been set, and bishoprics were created, first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a> around 1165, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a> after the council of 1167 at Saint-Félix-Lauragais.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Papal missions from 1147 onwards had little lasting effect. In January 1208 the papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-de-castelnau">Pierre de Castelnau</a> was murdered after excommunicating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-vi-count-of-toulouse">Raymond VI, Count of Toulouse</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> that followed besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> on 22 July 1209, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1229 ended the official war, leaving the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> dispossessed of much of their lands. Persecution by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> continued: Peire Autier, leader of a revival in the Pyrenean foothills, was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in April 1310, and the last known Perfect in Languedoc, Guillaume Bélibaste, in 1321.</p><p>Since the 1990s some historians, notably R. I. Moore, have questioned whether Catharism existed as an organised religion at all, arguing that churchmen largely constructed it from a broader spiritual revival. The region of Cathar castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> is now called Pays cathare. The Cathars have also been tied, without sound basis, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catharism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the mid-twelfth century the dualist <b>Cathar</b> movement spread among artisans, merchants and lesser nobles of the region, organising its own bishoprics and finding protection among lords who tolerated it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bogomil movement influenced dualist heresies as far west as the <b>Cathars</b> of southern France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Middle Ages Christian writers called later dualist movements, such as the Bogomils of the Balkans and the <b>Cathars</b> of southern France, “Manichaeans”, though historians doubt any direct descent.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In a reign of just three years Louis took Poitou from the English and led a royal crusade against the <b>Cathars</b> of the south.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-14">14 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Hunted by the Inquisition, the remaining <b>Cathars</b> became more and more scattered fugitives.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-16">16 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A small force made up mainly of <b>Cathars</b> massacred a group of inquisitors, the Dominican William Arnaud and eleven of his companions, during the Albigensian Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
